        <div id="recipes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Protein Smoothie Recipes, With Approximate Numbers
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are starting points, not lab-measured recipes. The calorie and protein figures are rough estimates from typical labels and will shift with your brand of powder, milk and fruit. For the smoothest result, add liquid first, then the powder, then soft ingredients, then frozen fruit and ice; that order lets the powder hydrate instead of sticking to the walls of the jar.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Berry Recovery Smoothie (Thin, Post-Workout)</h3>

          <p>
            One scoop of whey isolate or unflavored whey, one cup of unsweetened almond milk or water, one cup of frozen mixed berries and half a cup of ice. Blend for about 30 seconds. Roughly 200-250 calories and around 25 g of protein. Isolate keeps it drinkable and light, which makes this the one to have after training when you want something that will not sit heavily.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Peanut Butter Banana Smoothie (Creamy, Everyday)</h3>

          <p>
            One scoop of vanilla or chocolate whey, one cup of 2 percent milk, one frozen banana, one tablespoon of peanut butter and a pinch of cinnamon. Roughly 430-480 calories and around 35 g of protein, which makes it a solid breakfast. To lighten it, use unsweetened almond milk and half the peanut butter, which takes off roughly 120-150 calories and a few grams of protein.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Green Mango Smoothie (Dairy-Free)</h3>

          <p>
            One scoop of plant protein, either a pea blend or unflavored pea, one cup of unsweetened soy milk, one cup of frozen mango, a large handful of spinach and a squeeze of lime. Roughly 300-380 calories and around 25-35 g of protein, depending on the powder. Mango and lime cover the earthy note of pea well, and spinach adds color more than taste. Our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> rankings explain why plant proteins benefit from a slightly bigger scoop.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thick Chocolate Smoothie Bowl</h3>

          <p>
            One scoop of chocolate casein, about half a cup of milk, one frozen banana, a tablespoon of unsweetened cocoa and a handful of ice. Blend on low, scraping down often, until it is thick enough to hold a spoon upright, then top with a tablespoon of chia seeds and a few sliced strawberries. Roughly 350-400 calories and around 30 g of protein before extra toppings; a quarter cup of granola adds roughly another 110-150 calories.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Build the smoothie around the protein, not on top of it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Pick the protein type for texture, the flavor for the fruit, and measure the calorie-dense add-ins. One scoop, one or two servings of fruit and one measured fat source covers almost every goal, from a light recovery drink to a full breakfast.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="blending-tips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Blending Tips: Foam, Acid and Grit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Order matters more than most people expect. Powder dumped on top of frozen fruit can cake on the jar walls or form a dry pocket under the blades; liquid first, then powder, avoids both. If your blender is very powerful and you are using whey isolate, add the powder near the end and blend on low for the final few seconds to limit foam. Letting a frothy smoothie sit for a minute also lets the bubbles settle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some fruits react with dairy protein. Fresh pineapple contains bromelain and fresh kiwi contains actinidin, enzymes that break down protein; in a dairy smoothie left to sit, they can make it taste bitter and turn the texture thin or curdled. Drunk straight away it is usually fine, and canned pineapple, which has been heated, has little active enzyme left. Very acidic additions such as citrus juice can also make casein-rich drinks look curdled. Whey isolate copes with acidity much better, which is why clear, juice-style protein drinks are made from it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant proteins and some unflavored powders can feel slightly gritty or chalky. A longer blend, 45-60 seconds on high, helps, as do creamy ingredients like banana, avocado, yogurt or nut butter. If you make smoothies ahead, store them sealed in the fridge, drink them within about a day, and expect them to thicken, especially with casein, chia or oats; a quick shake or a few seconds of reblending fixes it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Freezer packs save the most time. Portion fruit, greens, oats and seeds into bags, keep the powder in its tub, and in the morning tip a pack into the blender with your liquid and a scoop for a protein smoothie in about two minutes. Blending does not damage protein nutritionally; the amino acids are just as available whether a powder is shaken, blended or stirred.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="goal-diet-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Choosing for Your Goal, Diet and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Match the powder to the job. For a post-workout smoothie, whey isolate or a whey blend gives a fast, leucine-rich dose with few added calories. For breakfast or meal-style smoothies, a whey blend or casein with oats and fruit gives a more filling drink that holds you longer. For bowls, casein or a thick plant blend is the right base. Use our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> to set a daily target, then decide how much of it the smoothie should cover.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dairy-free smoothie makers have it easier than shaker users, because fruit and plant milk cover the earthy taste and grit of pea protein far better than water does. A pea and rice blend or a single-source pea protein both work well; plan on a slightly larger serving, around 30 g of protein, to match the leucine in a 25 g serving of whey. Our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> comparison covers the trade-offs in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are worth a thought for smoothie drinkers specifically, because two common smoothie ingredients, cocoa and plant protein, are among the more frequent sources of lead and cadmium in independent testing of protein products. That is a reason to favor brands that publish third-party test results, not a reason to give up chocolate smoothies. Our guide on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> explains how to read those testing claims.
          </p>

          <p>
            A few groups should check with a doctor or registered dietitian first: anyone with kidney disease, people who are pregnant or breastfeeding, people with a milk or soy allergy, and anyone managing blood sugar with medication, where a fruit-heavy smoothie can have a bigger effect than expected. This guide is general information, not medical advice. For most people, a scoop of good protein in a smoothie is one of the easiest ways to hit a daily target, and our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> will help you set it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Dessert Flavor for Fruit Smoothies</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cookies and cream, birthday cake and caramel powders can taste great in milk and still clash with berries, mango or greens. They also carry the heaviest sweetener loads, which stack with fruit sugar. If you mostly make smoothies, buy unflavored or vanilla and add cocoa, cinnamon or nut butter yourself when you want a dessert-style blend.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Whey Isolate for a Smoothie Bowl</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whey isolate is thin and foamy, so a bowl built on it turns into a drink that will not hold toppings. For a spoonable bowl, use casein, a plant blend or Greek yogurt, cut the liquid back, use frozen fruit, and blend on low while scraping down the jar often until it is thick.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Counting the Add-Ins</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The scoop of powder is usually the smallest part of a smoothie's calories. Nut butter, oats, granola, honey, juice and whole milk add up fast, and a big smoothie can pass 700 calories. That is fine for weight gain or a full meal, but if you are cutting, measure the calorie-dense ingredients rather than pouring them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Doubling Up on Sweetness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pairing a heavily sweetened powder with two bananas, mango and a splash of juice produces a drink many people find sickly sweet. Pick one source of sweetness to lead. Use unflavored powder with sweet fruit, or a flavored powder balanced with unsweetened milk, tart berries, greens or plain yogurt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skimping on Plant Protein Dose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some plant powders deliver only 15-20 g of protein per scoop, with less leucine per gram than whey. In a recovery smoothie, aim for about 25-30 g of plant protein by choosing a higher-protein powder, using a slightly larger scoop, or adding soy milk or a protein-rich plant yogurt to the blend.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder for smoothies?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people, an unflavored or vanilla whey works best. Naked Whey is our top pick because it is a single-ingredient whey concentrate that lets the fruit lead, while Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard in vanilla is the most versatile flavored option. For thick smoothie bowls, casein is the better base, and for dairy-free smoothies, a pea or plant blend works well.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Should I use unflavored or flavored protein powder in smoothies?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Unflavored is the most flexible because it adds no sweetener and suits any fruit, but it relies on your other ingredients for flavor. Vanilla is the best flavored option because it complements almost every fruit and also works in a shaker. Avoid strong dessert flavors if you mainly make fruit smoothies, since they often clash and add a lot of sweetness.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which protein powder makes smoothies thicker?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Micellar casein thickens the most, followed by pea and other plant proteins, which absorb liquid and often contain gums or fiber. Whey concentrate gives a medium, creamy texture, and whey isolate stays thin. You can also thicken any smoothie with frozen fruit, less liquid, Greek yogurt, oats or chia seeds, which keep thickening as they sit.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein powder should I put in a smoothie?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  One standard scoop, usually 20-30 g of protein, is right for most adults. Research summarized by the International Society of Sports Nutrition points to roughly 20-40 g of protein per meal, or about 0.4 g per kg of body weight. Count the milk or yogurt in that total, and use a slightly larger serving of plant protein to match the leucine in whey.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I make a protein smoothie the night before?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Store it sealed in the fridge and drink it within about a day. Expect it to thicken, especially with casein, oats or chia, and to separate a little, so shake it or briefly reblend it. Avoid fresh pineapple or kiwi in make-ahead dairy smoothies, because their enzymes can turn the protein bitter and the texture thin as it sits.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are protein smoothies good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  They can be, if the smoothie replaces something rather than adding to your day and you watch the add-ins. A smoothie with one scoop of protein, unsweetened milk and one serving of fruit lands around 200-300 calories with 25 g or more of protein, which is filling for the calories. Versions with nut butter, granola and juice can easily triple that.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does blending destroy protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Blending does not reduce the nutritional value of protein. The mechanical action can partly unfold protein structures, which is part of why whey isolate foams, but the amino acids your body absorbs are the same. Digestion breaks proteins down far more thoroughly than any blender could. The only thing blending really changes is texture, such as foam and thickness.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
